WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
Speaker 1: Yes, Donald Trump is getting arrested&nbsp;
later. But we start today with economic news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.880
We'll get to this, the Trump stuff. We'll have&nbsp;
time to discuss it tomorrow. Let's start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:18.360
economic news. Inflation has now dropped to its&nbsp;
lowest point in two years. And as you all know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:24.300
I don't like to play politics with these economic&nbsp;
indicators. I don't play politics with debt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:31.860
deficit. We have to look at it for what it is. And&nbsp;
if you do that, you will then have no choice but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:39.480
to laugh at the way that Fox News covered this.&nbsp;
Yahoo Finance reports Inflation. Consumer prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:48.300
in May rose at slowest annual rate since April&nbsp;
of 2021. That's, in fact, more than two years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:56.100
It's 25, almost 26 months. The Consumer Price&nbsp;
Index revealed headline inflation rose 0.1%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:05.340
over the last month and 4% over the prior year&nbsp;
in May, a slowdown from April 0.4%, month over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:12.360
month increase and 4.9% annual gain. Both measures&nbsp;
roughly in line with economists forecasts. So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:18.540
have a chart that you can see and of course you&nbsp;
see this massive inflation spike that started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.580
right when COVID started. Essentially,&nbsp;
it has now come down to levels that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:28.440
are still higher than what the U.S. has&nbsp;
seen, better equivalent to what we saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:35.880
in 2011, lower than what we saw during the&nbsp;
recession in 2008, similar to numbers we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:42.120
had in 2006, similar to numbers in 2001, lower the&nbsp;
numbers in 91. So you start to see that we are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:47.400
in an inflation range that is a little bit higher&nbsp;
than what we have historically said is the target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:55.380
but are very reasonable inflation numbers. Is this&nbsp;
a surprise? It is not. I don't have any special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.900
predictive ability, but anybody paying attention&nbsp;
to the trends. We saw an increase in the rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:07.980
of inflation. We saw a decrease in the increase&nbsp;
of that rate. And then the next thing you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.500
expect is an actual decrease. My thought was that&nbsp;
by the end of this summer we would be somewhere in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:20.700
the 4 to 5 range. We may even once we get June's&nbsp;
numbers in July or July's numbers in August,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.980
we may actually see inflation down into the high&nbsp;
threes, which is even lower than I thought it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:32.400
might be, or it might stay level right around this&nbsp;
4%. Important to understand a couple of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.400
things, and I always explain these things. Number&nbsp;
one, when people see the number 4%, very often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:47.040
they think prices went up 4% in May. They did not.&nbsp;
4% is the inflation over the last 12 months. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:54.480
what happens when you get a new month's worth of&nbsp;
data is that in that year indicator, you drop that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.420
13 month ago number and you add the latest one.&nbsp;
So let me give you an example. The number we got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:10.440
last month in May. Told us what was inflation from&nbsp;
April 22 to April 23. Now that we have the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:19.140
numbers, we update the 12 month indicator and we&nbsp;
now know what was inflation from May 22 to May 23,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:26.280
it's a trailing 12 month indicator. Number&nbsp;
two, many people aren't aware that based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:32.460
standards that we have for our economies and how&nbsp;
we think about the economy, the goal is roughly 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:37.260
to 3% inflation. Now, you might say, David, why?&nbsp;
Why would we want inflation? And it's important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:43.080
to understand this. You can critique it by saying&nbsp;
this is not sustainable long term as an economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:49.020
because we can't have indefinite growth, which I&nbsp;
would engage with you on. But the reason that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:54.840
little bit of inflation is considered good is if&nbsp;
you have too much inflation, as we understand life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:00.480
becomes unaffordable for people. It can cause&nbsp;
spending to stop because people can't afford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.160
things and it can cause a recession. That's if&nbsp;
we have too much inflation. You might say, Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:11.880
but wouldn't deflation be good then? Prices going&nbsp;
down sounds good. People can afford things. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:18.360
start seeing deflation where things get cheaper.&nbsp;
It also causes people not to spend and businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:24.720
not to invest. Why would that be? If you expect&nbsp;
that things will be cheaper in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.060
either as an individual consumer thinking about&nbsp;
buying a car or as a business thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.560
doing an expansion, building a new warehouse,&nbsp;
or whatever the case may be. If you think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:41.520
will be cheaper in the future, you would wait.&nbsp;
That waiting is a decline in economic activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.560
which then trickles down and can lead to layoffs.&nbsp;
And okay, so that's what we call a deflationary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:52.260
spiral. That's why a little bit of inflation is&nbsp;
considered good. That little bit of inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:58.680
is what encourages people to buy now rather than&nbsp;
later. But they can still afford right now. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.060
often a little bit of inflation is in parallel&nbsp;
with wage growth. So that's like the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:09.000
economic view. Now, how did Fox News react to&nbsp;
this news? Well, they think there may be too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.940
many jobs. They are pulling the labor shortage&nbsp;
line. This is Maria BARTIROMO and Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:19.860
Congressman Buddy Carter. Just this morning.
