Speaker 1: Yes. All right. These people really are&nbsp;
pathetic. The argument he's making is there are so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.840
many businesses desperate to hire people that it&nbsp;
is a terrible economy. The economy must be doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:41.700
terribly because so many businesses are saying,&nbsp;
hey, let's hire because we have demand for our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:47.340
products and services. Now, let I'll be very clear&nbsp;
with you. Let's talk about labor shortage. A labor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:52.380
shortage happens when demand for workers&nbsp;
in a particular industry, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.380 --> 00:07:00.960
exceeds the supply of workers available with the&nbsp;
skills required willing to work at the wage rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:07.440
that is being offered. Labor shortages can be a&nbsp;
sign of a good economy or they can be troubling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.960
Labor shortages can have positive or negative&nbsp;
effects on an economy depending on the type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:20.880
labor shortage, the duration, the severity. And&nbsp;
so a labor shortage, even if that's what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:26.460
are calling it, is not necessarily a bad thing&nbsp;
for the economy. It might just signal really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:31.680
strong demand for goods and services. It might&nbsp;
create incentives to innovate and to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:38.340
efficiency. And it might mean that employers&nbsp;
need to offer higher wages. They love talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:43.920
